{
  "image_name": "2b378eb30231c675558fb13688a9cab8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how gut microbial metabolism of diet can generate signaling molecules with effects on immunity, metabolism, and disease. A major pathway shows dietary fibre being processed by microbial enzymes into short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs). These SCFAs act on adipocytes, immune cells, and intestinal epithelial cells, where they inhibit HDACs or signal through receptors such as GPR41/43. The visible outcomes include immunomodulation, epithelial nourishment, release of hormones like GLP1 and PYY, regulation of food intake and energy expenditure, and effects on IL-18 and inflammation.\n\nOther dietary substrates are linked to different microbial metabolites with more disease-oriented consequences. L-carnitine, choline, and phosphatidylcholine lead to TMAO, which is connected to arteries and cardiovascular disease. Tryptophan produces indole derivatives that appear to act through epithelial PXR, immune-cell AHR, and GLP1-related pathways to shape immune responses. Phenylalanine gives phenylacetate, and alcohol gives acetaldehyde; both converge on the liver and are associated with NASH and fibrosis. Tyrosine produces 4EPS, which is tentatively linked to autism. Overall, the image contrasts beneficial microbial products such as SCFAs with metabolites associated with inflammatory, cardiovascular, hepatic, and possibly neurodevelopmental outcomes.",
